\description{
Implements the \emph{exact} (analytical) version of the NIRA intervention
analysis.  Instead of generating Monte Carlo samples with
\code{nodeIdentifyR::simulateResponses}, the mean number of activated nodes
is computed \strong{exactly} by enumerating all \eqn{2^p} activation
microstates of the Ising model (Cui et al., 2026).  The analytical solution
eliminates Monte Carlo error, so the only remaining source of uncertainty is
\strong{sampling error} — the network parameters are estimated from a finite
sample.  Accordingly, uncertainty is quantified by \strong{resampling the
original dataset} (not simulated samples) and re-computing the analytical
solution on each resampled network.

Uncertainty is quantified by \strong{resampling the original dataset}
(rows resampled with replacement, the Ising network re-estimated, and the
exact solution re-computed on every resample).  A single resampling loop
simultaneously produces two tests, which answer different questions:
\describe{
  \item{\strong{Bootstrap test} (in \code{stat})}{Two-sided bootstrap
    \eqn{p}-value for \eqn{H_0: \Delta_i = 0} (the analytical analogue of
    the Monte-Carlo permutation test in \code{\link{permutationNIRAtest}}),
    standard errors, 95 \% percentile confidence intervals, and the
    proportion of resamples in which each node had the largest absolute
    intervention effect (\code{percenttop_1}, the analytical analogue of
    \code{\link{findMaxN}}).}
  \item{\strong{Random-target test} (in \code{random})}{The null is
    generated by \strong{permuting the assignment of the intervention
    across nodes}: within each resample the effect of node \eqn{i} is
    compared with the effects of the \eqn{p-1} other (randomly assigned)
    target nodes.  The \eqn{p}-value tests \eqn{H_0:} node \eqn{i} is
    \emph{not} a better intervention target than a randomly chosen node.
    It is reported \strong{without} multiple-comparison correction.
    Because the analytical solution makes every single-node effect
    deterministically non-zero, this ranking question (rather than
    \eqn{\Delta_i = 0}) is the meaningful one for target selection.}
}
}
\section{Method notes}{
\itemize{
  \item The exact computation transforms the \eqn{\{0,1\}}-coded Ising
    parameters into the spin (\eqn{\pm 1}) convention and enumerates all
    \eqn{2^p} microstates \strong{once per network} with a numerically
    stable log-sum-exp scheme.  A one-node intervention changes only the
    single-node field, so the perturbed mean activation is obtained by
    exactly reweighting the microstates by \eqn{e^{\Delta_i v_i}} — no
    re-enumeration of the \eqn{2^p} states is needed for the \eqn{p}
    interventions.  The result is identical to
    \code{Isinglandr::make_2d_Isingland(transform = TRUE)}.
  \item The intervention magnitude is fixed at
    \code{amount_of_SDs_perturbation} \eqn{\times} \code{sd(thresholds)}
    (the point-estimate SD) across all resamples, so the resampled effects
    reflect sampling error in the network structure only.
  \item The resampling loop re-estimates the network with a saturated
    nodewise logistic-regression (pseudo-likelihood) estimator, which is
    substantially faster than \code{bootnet::estimateNetwork} but performs
    no model selection.  For the point estimate, either provide parameters
    from your preferred estimator (e.g., \code{bootnet}) or accept the
    default fast estimator.
}
}
\examples{
## Example 1: exact analysis + bootstrap inference (fast demo)
\dontrun{
data("single_gds", package = "NIRApost")

res <- analyticalNIRAtest(
  data              = single_gds,
  perturbation_type = "aggravating",
  nResample         = 100,   # 5000 recommended for publication
  seed              = 2025
)
res$stat
res$plot
}

## Example 2: reproduce the paper's exact Fig. 5 point estimates by
## supplying the bootnet IsingFit parameters; both bootstrap and
## permutation results are returned together
\dontrun{
fit <- bootnet::estimateNetwork(single_gds, default = "IsingFit",
                                rule = "AND")
res <- analyticalNIRAtest(
  data              = single_gds,
  edge_weights      = fit$results$weiadj,
  thresholds        = fit$results$thresholds,
  perturbation_type = "aggravating",
  nResample         = 5000,
  parallel          = TRUE
)
head(res$stat)        # bootstrap-based inference (SE, CI, p, percenttop_1)
res$random            # target-vs-random p-values (uncorrected)
}

## Example 3: exact-only (no resampling)
\dontrun{
res0 <- analyticalNIRAtest(single_gds, "aggravating", nResample = 0)
res0$exact
}
